Cell["\<\
Use the cubic QED model for every diagram calculation and for four-point \
factorization. The model containing known four- and five-point amplitudes \
supplies five- and six-point factorization. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  4.003241769285503*^9},ExpressionUUID->"e40be2b8-a07c-4c47-93ff-\
40449897d0a0"],

Cell["\<\
diagramModel = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"models\"}], 2];
factorizationModel = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"models\"}], 3];
factorizationModelSpurious = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \
\"models\"}], 4];
\
\>", "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.002815856960981*^9, 4.002815864610408*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[4]:=",ExpressionUUID->"f61894d2-c74b-4610-a3de-e1941760c4ae"],

Cell["\<\
The independent benchmark uses the massive-QED Feynman model. Its own model \
file specifies the same numerical electron mass and charge as the \
constructive QED example. Each model retains its own parameters; no global \
parameter assignments or symbol substitutions are needed.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5e949351-7167-4e81-83a5-e5d0eebf69c2"],

Cell["\<\
qedFeynmanModel = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \
\"tests\", \"reference-models\", \"calchep\"}], 2];\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[8]:=",ExpressionUUID->"39ecf8f2-3357-45c7-b69b-99fa03763f4d"],

Cell["\<\
The amplitudes retain symbolic Me and EE. Numerical checks and completion use \
the parameter values and derived functions from the supplied model. PASS \
means agreement at the tested samples, not a general analytic proof.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"43f1ccea-2be0-4dac-abdd-f6a191035779"],

Cell["\<\
The construction wrapper chooses its calculation settings automatically, \
reports progress and elapsed time, and returns the expression only when all \
required checks pass. The one option shown below supplies the independent \
Feynman benchmark model; it does not tune the calculation. Other options are \
needed only to override a default.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ec7e2a65-5ad5-54a8-b037-0c605814a7c6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["C++ Output", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"b4c6fb87-9ca2-562d-8135-105e4be086dd"],

Cell["\<\
The export cells at the end of each related amplitude family write one SPINAS \
process containing its requested spin and helicity choices. Evaluate the \
amplitude calculations first. Equivalent photon placements are supplied by \
relabeling identical particles. The generated amp selects one spin/helicity \
configuration; spin_sum sums its squared magnitude over all external states, \
and amp2 also includes the initial-state spin average and final-state \
identical-particle factor. Each export builds and runs three example \
phase-space points. Failed or unevaluated amplitudes stop the export before \
files are written. The process arrow separates incoming and outgoing \
particles. A generic A sums photon helicities; A.+ fixes a helicity on either \
side. Supply the original all-ingoing expressions: outgoing momenta and angle \
spinors are crossed automatically. Fixed incoming helicities are not \
averaged. The output folder is set below. Existing project files are \
protected; add \"Overwrite\" -> True only when you want to replace a previous \
export.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"434e8d10-f994-50ec-b513-28ad794a07e5"],

Cell["\<\
spinasOutputDirectory = FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory,\"tests\", \
\"spinas-output\"}];\
\>", "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0023639741745567`*^9, 4.002363979610005*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[9]:=",ExpressionUUID->"bd44a057-8980-5237-a7d8-0b841566c656"],

Cell["\<\
Choose the independent reference programs and models here. These are explicit \
example locations; change them for your installation. The selected MadGraph \
SM model is restricted to electrons, positrons and photons by the visible PDG \
list. CalcHEP uses the separate massive-QED Feynman model. The checks compare \
squared amplitudes, so overall phases are allowed. Each individual \
calculation has a MadGraph check at its specified photon helicities, with \
massive spins summed. Each grouped C++ export also runs the full CalcHEP \
spin/helicity-summed comparison and the MadGraph helicity checks. All use \
1000 random points by default and print the actual seed for replay. Failed or \
unfinished constructive calculations are never counted as passing.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"26c8f871-c0fb-5a55-a4c8-8bc257b7184b"],

Cell["\<\
madGraphDirectory = FileNameJoin[{ParentDirectory[hepcatDirectory], \
\"MadGraph\"}];
madGraphModel = \"sm-full\";
calcHEPDirectory = FileNameJoin[{ParentDirectory[hepcatDirectory], \
\"CalcHEP\", \"calchep_3.9.2\"}];
calcHEPModel = {FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"tests\", \"reference-models\", \"calchep\"}], 2};
referenceParticlePDGs = {11, 22};
validationPoints = 1000;
madGraphValidationOptions = {
  \"MadGraphDirectory\" -> madGraphDirectory,
  \"MadGraphModel\" -> madGraphModel,
  \"MadGraphAllowedParticles\" -> referenceParticlePDGs,
  \"MadGraphPoints\" -> validationPoints
};\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[10]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c44af2bb-0ad6-562d-8a65-8ae930fd2c08"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Four-Point Amplitudes", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"058277b2-d0a3-505b-bd1a-c27c47ce0997"],

Cell["\<\
Five separate calculations: every two-photon helicity assignment and \
four-fermion scattering. Four-point factorization uses the same cubic model \
as the diagrams.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b73eb392-82ee-5711-a971-c472ac8ca249"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["e,E,A.+,A.+", "Chapter",ExpressionUUID->"44abe297-98a2-5c63-9e22-20132a3d21cb"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
qed4PP = ConstructAmplitude[
  {\"e\", \"E\", \"A.+\", \"A.+\"},
  diagramModel, diagramModel,
  \"FeynmanModel\" -> qedFeynmanModel]\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[17]:=",ExpressionUUID->"90196480-cbbe-591b-86bf-9971ced0f0e4"],

